From b3adf150664e0f88193e3e561bf3a36252897a58 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 04:34:23 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 208/213] feat: ShadePS shades a ray traced hit by its material
 type - vegetation, skin, glass, water (decision 10)

The hit shading had one branch: CE's standard opaque forward lobe, plus a grey 0.5 transmittance
for anything whose ALD.w sign said "vegetation". The HITMAT lane now carries the real tag and the
real per type parameters, so each type takes the path the shader it is named after takes.

0 Illum and 5 Terrain: unchanged, the stock sum. Terrain deliberately gets no branch of its own -
Terrain.cfx's lighting IS the Illum lobe, and the record carries no terrain normal map to apply.

0.25 vegetation leaves and 2 human skin: CE's one forward answer to light coming through a
surface, LIGHTINGMODEL_TRANSMITTANCE, which makes Callback_ShadingDirect call ThinTranslucencyBRDF
(shadeLib.cfi:952-964) INSTEAD OF the diffuse BRDF - a substitution, not an addition, which is why
a leaf is not brighter than the trunk of the same tree. What changes is the colour: the material's
own, resolved per texel at the hit, instead of grey 0.5. Vegetation.cfx:503-512 gives
saturate(BackDiffuseMultiplier * translucencyMap) * TransmittanceColor (default 1, 1, 0.6);
HumanSkin.cfx:286-298 gives exp((1 - saturate(mask * TranslucencyMultiplier)) * (-8, -40, -64)),
which is the red glow. CustomForwardShadingAttribs.Transmittance had to widen from a float to a
float3 for that - it was broadcast to grey, which is the one thing a transmittance colour must not
be. A leaf record that predates the extras still gets the old 0.5.

Skin is the forward approximation and nothing more, as decision 10 says: CE's skin is this
lighting model plus a SCREEN SPACE subsurface blur keyed on attribs.ScatteringIndex
(HumanSkin.cfx:288-293). There is no screen at an off screen hit, so the blur cannot be
reproduced; the transmittance term, which is the part that makes an ear glow, is.

3 glass: Glass.cfx:575-586 shades a pane from its TINT, not its albedo, so the tint replaces the
albedo and the composite is Glass's own,
    cFinal = lerp(lerp(cBackbuffer * tint, tint * diffuseAcc, TintCloudiness), litDiffuse,
                  backlightMask), then lerp(cDiffuse, cSpecular, fresnel)
with two terms collapsed. cBackbuffer - what is behind the pane - is not a value this branch needs:
the traversal's stipple acceptance already sends the ray straight through the pane in (1 - opacity)
of the four stipple phases, so the background arrives AS those other phases and the stage 4B
temporal filter does that lerp for us. backlightMask is a forward only screen term and is 0. What
is left is the cloudy half - the tint lit as a diffuse - and the specular, combined by
GetEnvmapFresnel, the same function Glass.cfx:579 uses.

4 water: decision 09 section 9.4 and the record the stage 5C producer writes. The body colour
(fog colour attenuated by exp2(-density * depth), Water.cfx:1116-1117, with what is under the
water taken as fully absorbed because v1 sends no refraction ray) takes the albedo's place, so the
sun and the SVO GI light it like any other diffuse surface. The reflection is Water.cfx:1160,
    cFinal = lerp(refractColor, cSpecularAcc, fFresnel)
with cSpecularAcc replaced by the SAME sky lookup the miss path already has, evaluated along the
mirror of the incoming ray about the water normal and scaled by ReflectionScale - no secondary ray
in v1. fFresnel is GetEnvmapFresnel on the record's F0, which the producer writes as water's
0.02 (Water.cfx:1134). The sun highlight rides on top, as SunSpecular does (Water.cfx:971-992).
When the sky answers, water passes bSkipSpecularProbes so the probe specular does not double the
reflection it is an estimate of; a ray reflected downward off water gets nothing from a lat-long
dome and keeps the ordinary probe specular instead. That flag is a new defaulted parameter on
Fwd_StandardOpaqueShading_Raytracing, so every other call site is textually unchanged.

Alpha blended Illum materials need no code at all - the traversal's stipple test already accepts
them with probability equal to their opacity, which is the same stochastic rule glass uses.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01SCUvWGLCsqzm3VNMzp4uiS
---
 .../HWScripts/CryFX/Total_Illumination.cfx    | 116 +++++++++++++++++-
 .../CryFX/Total_Illumination_Shading.cfi      |  10 +-
 2 files changed, 117 insertions(+), 9 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Engine/Shaders/HWScripts/CryFX/Total_Illumination.cfx b/Engine/Shaders/HWScripts/CryFX/Total_Illumination.cfx
index 6cf2a4ce..447eb679 100644
--- a/Engine/Shaders/HWScripts/CryFX/Total_Illumination.cfx
+++ b/Engine/Shaders/HWScripts/CryFX/Total_Illumination.cfx
@@ -13,7 +13,12 @@
 struct CustomForwardShadingAttribs
 {
 	int    LightingModel;
-	float  Transmittance;
+	// rt stage 5B: a float3, not a scalar. ThinTranslucencyBRDF takes a transmittance COLOUR
+	// (shadeLib.cfi:952) and both CE sources of one are coloured - Vegetation's
+	// TransmittanceColor defaults to (1, 1, 0.6) and HumanSkin's is exp(x * (-8, -40, -64)),
+	// which is the red skin glow. Until now this lane was one scalar broadcast to grey, which
+	// is the one thing a transmittance colour must not be.
+	float3 Transmittance;
 	float  Emittance;
 	float3 RayDirection;
 	float  SunShadowRT;
@@ -2240,18 +2245,54 @@ pixoutShade ShadePS(vtxOutWPOS IN)
 
 		const float3 vEmmTint = RT_UnpackEmissiveHue(vGAL.w);
 
+		// rt stage 5B (decision 10): the hit's material TYPE and its per type parameters.
+		uint nMatCode; float fMatParam; bool bMatExtras;
+		RT_UnpackHitMatCode(refl_GMAT.SampleLevel(ssSvoPointClamp, IN.baseTC.xy, 0).x, nMatCode, fMatParam, bMatExtras);
+		const float3 vMatRgb = refl_GMAT.SampleLevel(ssSvoPointClamp, IN.baseTC.xy, 0).yzw;
+
+		// The sign of the packed emissive hue is stage 2D's veg-leaves marker and is kept as the
+		// fallback, so a record written before the tag existed still gets the two-sided model.
+		const bool bTagVegLeaves = (nMatCode == VOX_RT_MATCODE_VEG_LEAVES) || (vGAL.w < 0);
+		const bool bTagSkin      = (nMatCode == VOX_RT_MATCODE_HUMAN_SKIN);
+		const bool bTagGlass     = (nMatCode == VOX_RT_MATCODE_GLASS);
+		const bool bTagWater     = (nMatCode == VOX_RT_MATCODE_WATER);
+
 		MaterialAttribsCommon commonAttribs = (MaterialAttribsCommon)0;
 		commonAttribs.NormalWorld = normalize(vGAL.xyz);
 		commonAttribs.Albedo      = vGNO.xyz;
 		commonAttribs.Reflectance = vReflectance;
 		commonAttribs.Smoothness  = fSmoothness;
 
-		if (vGAL.w < 0) // the lighting model rides in the sign of the packed emissive hue
+		// VEGETATION LEAVES (tag 0.25) and HUMAN SKIN (tag 2) share CE's one forward answer to
+		// "light comes through this surface": LIGHTINGMODEL_TRANSMITTANCE, which makes
+		// Callback_ShadingDirect call ThinTranslucencyBRDF (shadeLib.cfi:952-964) INSTEAD OF the
+		// diffuse BRDF - it is a substitution, not an addition, which is why the leaf is not
+		// twice as bright as the trunk. The colour is the material's own now:
+		//   Vegetation.cfx:503-512  saturate(BackDiffuseMultiplier * translucencyMap) * TransmittanceColor
+		//   HumanSkin.cfx:286-298   exp((1 - saturate(translucencyMap * TranslucencyMultiplier)) * (-8, -40, -64))
+		// Both are resolved per texel at the hit (RT_ProcessBestHit) and arrive in the HITMAT
+		// float3. A leaf material whose record predates the extras keeps the old grey 0.5.
+		//
+		// SKIN IS THE FORWARD APPROXIMATION AND NOTHING MORE. CE's skin is
+		// LIGHTINGMODEL_TRANSMITTANCE plus a SCREEN SPACE subsurface blur keyed on
+		// attribs.ScatteringIndex (HumanSkin.cfx:288-293, resolved by the SSS stage). There is
+		// no screen at a ray hit, so the blur cannot be reproduced; what is reproduced is the
+		// transmittance term, which is the part that makes an ear glow red from behind.
+		if (bTagVegLeaves || bTagSkin)
 		{
 			commonAttribs.LightingModel = LIGHTINGMODEL_TRANSMITTANCE;
-			commonAttribs.Transmittance = (half3)0.5f;
+			commonAttribs.Transmittance = bMatExtras ? (half3)vMatRgb : (half3)0.5f;
 		}
 
+		// GLASS (tag 3): Glass.cfx:575-586 shades the pane from its TINT, not from its albedo -
+		// localTintColor is what the glass keeps of what is behind it, and the albedo only
+		// appears through the backlight mask, which the hit has no equivalent of.
+		// WATER (tag 4): the body colour computed at the hit (fog colour attenuated by
+		// density x depth, Water.cfx:1116-1117) takes the albedo's place, so the sun and the
+		// SVO GI light the water body the same way they light any other diffuse surface.
+		if (bTagGlass || bTagWater)
+			commonAttribs.Albedo = vMatRgb;
+
 		ForwardShadingAttribs fwdAttribs = (ForwardShadingAttribs)0;
 		fwdAttribs.screenUV        = IN.baseTC.xy;
 		fwdAttribs.worldPos        = vGPO.xyz; // camera-relative, as everywhere in the forward stack
@@ -2262,7 +2303,8 @@ pixoutShade ShadePS(vtxOutWPOS IN)
 		fwdAttribs.smoothness      = commonAttribs.Smoothness;
 
 		fwdAttribs.custom.LightingModel = commonAttribs.LightingModel;
-		fwdAttribs.custom.Transmittance = (commonAttribs.LightingModel == LIGHTINGMODEL_TRANSMITTANCE) ? 0.5f : 0.f;
+		fwdAttribs.custom.Transmittance = (commonAttribs.LightingModel == LIGHTINGMODEL_TRANSMITTANCE)
+		                                  ? (float3)commonAttribs.Transmittance : (float3)0.f;
 		// emittance rides in the LENGTH of the packed normal
 		fwdAttribs.custom.Emittance     = max(length(vGAL.xyz) - 1.f, 0.f);
 		fwdAttribs.custom.RayDirection  = bNoHit ? -vRayDirN : vRayDirN;
@@ -2283,6 +2325,24 @@ pixoutShade ShadePS(vtxOutWPOS IN)
 		float3 vSkyRadiance = (float3)0.f;
 		const bool bMissSky = bNoHit && (vGLD.z > 0.f) && SVO_RT_SampleSky(vRayDirN, vSkyRadiance);
 
+		// rt stage 5B, WATER (decision 09 section 9.4, decision 10 item 4 Water). What a water
+		// surface shows is Water.cfx:1160
+		//     cFinal = lerp(refractColor, cSpecularAcc, fFresnel)
+		// with cSpecularAcc the reflection (Water.cfx:1053, a cubemap or the planar reflection,
+		// times ReflectionScale) and fFresnel = GetEnvmapFresnel(0.02, FresnelGloss, NdotE)
+		// (Water.cfx:1134-1135). v1 sends NO secondary ray: the reflection is the SAME sky
+		// lookup the miss path already has, evaluated along the mirror of the incoming ray about
+		// the water normal. A ray reflected DOWNWARD off water gets nothing from a lat-long
+		// dome, so it keeps the ordinary probe specular instead.
+		float3 vWaterRefl = (float3)0.f;
+		bool bWaterSky = false;
+
+		if (bTagWater && !bNoHit)
+		{
+			const float3 vWaterReflDir = reflect(vRayDirN, commonAttribs.NormalWorld);
+			bWaterSky = (vWaterReflDir.z > 0.f) && SVO_RT_SampleSky(vWaterReflDir, vWaterRefl);
+		}
+
 		float3 diffuseAcc = 0.0f;
 		float3 specularAcc = 0.0f;
 		SRTShadeProbe dbgShade = (SRTShadeProbe)0;
@@ -2295,12 +2355,56 @@ pixoutShade ShadePS(vtxOutWPOS IN)
 		// Fwd_StandardOpaqueShading_Raytracing where ApplyGI would have done it.
 		const float4 vGGI = refl_GGI.SampleLevel(ssSvoPointClamp, IN.baseTC.xy, 0);
 
+		// Water skips the SPECULAR PROBE when the sky answered: the probe specular and the sky
+		// lookup below are two estimates of the same reflection and adding both doubles it.
 		if (!bMissSky)
-			Fwd_StandardOpaqueShading_Raytracing(commonAttribs, fwdAttribs, diffuseAcc, specularAcc, dbgShade, true, !bNoHit, !bNoHit, vGGI);
+			Fwd_StandardOpaqueShading_Raytracing(commonAttribs, fwdAttribs, diffuseAcc, specularAcc, dbgShade, true, !bNoHit, !bNoHit, vGGI, bWaterSky);
+
+		// rt stage 5B: the per tag composite. Tags 0 (Illum) and 5 (Terrain) - and 0.25 and 2,
+		// whose whole difference is the lighting model the BRDF callback already switched on -
+		// take the stock sum. Terrain deliberately has no branch: Terrain.cfx's own lighting IS
+		// the Illum lobe, and the record carries no terrain normal map to apply.
+		float3 vShaded = diffuseAcc + specularAcc;
+
+		if (bTagGlass || bTagWater)
+		{
+			// GetEnvmapFresnel is what BOTH shaders use for this lerp: Glass.cfx:579 on the
+			// material's own reflectance, Water.cfx:1135 on a hard coded 0.02 with FresnelGloss.
+			// The hit's reflectance lane already IS that 0.02 for water (the CPU writes it) and
+			// the material's own F0 for glass, so one expression covers both.
+			const float3 vViewVec = normalize(-fwdAttribs.custom.RayDirection);
+			const float fFres = GetEnvmapFresnel(fwdAttribs.reflectance, fwdAttribs.smoothness,
+			                                     saturate(dot(commonAttribs.NormalWorld, vViewVec))).x;
+
+			if (bTagGlass)
+			{
+				// Glass.cfx:576-586:
+				//   tintBlend = lerp(cBackbuffer * tint, tint * diffuseAcc, TintCloudiness)
+				//   cDiffuse  = lerp(tintBlend, litDiffuse, backlightMask)
+				//   cFinal    = lerp(cDiffuse, cSpecular, fresnel)
+				// cBackbuffer - what is BEHIND the pane - is not a value this branch has, and it
+				// does not need one: the stochastic acceptance in the traversal already sends
+				// the ray straight through the pane in (1 - opacity) of the stipple phases, so
+				// the background half of tintBlend arrives as those other phases and the
+				// temporal filter of stage 4B does the lerp. What is left here is the cloudy
+				// half - the tint lit as a diffuse - and the specular, combined by CE's own
+				// Fresnel. backlightMask is a forward-only screen term and is taken as 0.
+				vShaded = lerp(diffuseAcc * fMatParam, specularAcc, fFres);
+			}
+			else if (bWaterSky)
+			{
+				// Water.cfx:1160, with refractColor collapsed to the lit body colour (v1 sends
+				// no refraction ray) and cSpecularAcc replaced by the sky along the reflected
+				// direction times ReflectionScale. specularAcc is the SUN highlight, which
+				// Water.cfx adds on top of the reflection in the same way (SunSpecular,
+				// Water.cfx:971-992).
+				vShaded = lerp(diffuseAcc, vWaterRefl * fMatParam, fFres) + specularAcc;
+			}
+		}
 
 		float3 acc = bMissSky
 		             ? (vSkyRadiance + .0001f)
-		             : (diffuseAcc + specularAcc + SVO_ShadeParams0.x * fwdAttribs.albedo + .0001f);
+		             : (vShaded + SVO_ShadeParams0.x * fwdAttribs.albedo + .0001f);
 
 		// Emissive, exactly Illum.cfx IlluminationPS:
 		//     emittance = MatEmissiveColor.rgb * MatEmissiveColor.w * MAT_EMISSIVE_UNIT_SCALE
diff --git a/Engine/Shaders/HWScripts/CryFX/Total_Illumination_Shading.cfi b/Engine/Shaders/HWScripts/CryFX/Total_Illumination_Shading.cfi
index 770b28ee..70fb9165 100644
--- a/Engine/Shaders/HWScripts/CryFX/Total_Illumination_Shading.cfi
+++ b/Engine/Shaders/HWScripts/CryFX/Total_Illumination_Shading.cfi
@@ -417,7 +417,8 @@ void TiledForwardShading_Raytracing(in ForwardShadingAttribs attribs, inout floa
 void Fwd_StandardOpaqueShading_Raytracing(in MaterialAttribsCommon commonAttribs, inout ForwardShadingAttribs fwdAttribs,
                                           inout float3 diffuseAcc, inout float3 specularAcc, out SRTShadeProbe dbg,
                                           bool bSunShadows = true, bool bDirectLights = true,
-                                          bool bUseHitGI = false, float4 vHitGI = (float4)0.f)
+                                          bool bUseHitGI = false, float4 vHitGI = (float4)0.f,
+                                          bool bSkipSpecularProbes = false)
 {
 	dbg = (SRTShadeProbe)0;
 	dbg.sunVisibility = 1;
@@ -458,8 +459,11 @@ void Fwd_StandardOpaqueShading_Raytracing(in MaterialAttribsCommon commonAttribs
 		dbg.directSpec += sunSpec;
 	}
 
-	// unconditional: %_RT_TILED_SHADING means "trace voxels" on this shader
-	TiledForwardShading_Raytracing(fwdAttribs, diffuseAcc, specularAcc, ssFwdBilinearWrap, ssFwdComparison, true, bDirectLights, false, dbg, bUseHitGI, vHitGI);
+	// unconditional: %_RT_TILED_SHADING means "trace voxels" on this shader.
+	// rt stage 5B: bSkipSpecularProbes was always false here; WATER passes true when the sky
+	// answered along the reflected direction, because the probe specular and that sky lookup are
+	// two estimates of the same reflection (decision 09 section 9.4).
+	TiledForwardShading_Raytracing(fwdAttribs, diffuseAcc, specularAcc, ssFwdBilinearWrap, ssFwdComparison, true, bDirectLights, bSkipSpecularProbes, dbg, bUseHitGI, vHitGI);
 
 	// energy audit error 1 / E1. The indirect DIFFUSE at the hit, taking the place the probe
 	// diffuse just vacated: the SVO's own voxel irradiance, which is exactly what ApplyGI mode 2
